## Migration: `EventProcessor` → .NET 8

### Problems with the original code on .NET 8

| API | Issue |
|-----|-------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable for `WaitOne` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` without cancellation | No way to wake cooperatively |
| Race condition | Multiple `Enqueue` calls can signal before items are drained |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny wakes on either a new event OR cancellation
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, token.WaitHandle });

            if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            // Drain all queued items (fixes the original race condition)
            while (true)
            {
                string? item;
                lock (_lock)
                {
                    if (_queue.Count == 0) break;
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
                }
                Handle(item);
            }

            _newEvent.Reset();
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();            // signals the token → wakes WaitAny
        _thread?.Join(5000);      // wait for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback needed — the loop checks the token
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

### Key Changes Explained

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative wake via `WaitHandle.WaitAny` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Removed | Loop exits naturally on `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `ManualResetEvent` | `ManualResetEventSlim` + `WaitHandle.WaitAny` | Lighter primitive; cancellation-aware wait |
| `while (true)` | `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)` | Cooperative exit |
| Dequeue single item | Drain loop | Fixes race where multiple enqueues share one signal |

---

### Common Pitfalls

1. **Forgetting `WaitHandle.WaitAny`** — Using `_newEvent.Wait(token)` on `ManualResetEventSlim` throws `OperationCanceledException` which you'd need to catch. `WaitAny` avoids exception-driven control flow.

2. **Not draining the queue** — The original code dequeues one item per signal. If two `Enqueue` calls happen before `WaitOne` returns, the second item is lost until the next signal. The migrated version drains all items.

3. **Stop() called before Start()** — Add a null check on `_thread` (handled with `_thread?.Join`).

4. **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** — Leaks internal timer resources. Always implement `IDisposable`.

5. **Assuming cancellation is instant** — If `Handle(item)` is long-running, cancellation waits for it to finish. For finer-grained cancellation, pass `_cts.Token` into `Handle` and check periodically.

---

### Even simpler: Use `BlockingCollection<T>`

If you don't need the manual threading, .NET has a purpose-built type:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
                Handle(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* expected on shutdown */ }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _thread?.Join(5000);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _queue.Dispose();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

`BlockingCollection<T>` + `GetConsumingEnumerable(CancellationToken)` replaces all the manual synchronization, eliminates the race condition, and responds to cancellation immediately when blocked.
